+OÑäú‹f]bc’éËøÍ‰¨[ÌĞ‡_r‡iğ×óôï°ÜQ‡>5*9ç}>‘ã²~Œs¾Âeõ7«]	åô¦™§1+œè™¿œí¥ı^É</Evyö¾¹Jmú™ïó»^Yôû¥‰À2­ïTÏìu™ß9LvõÜÅ-x=ÁYGy)Õb}DDD4(|B¹½µ/7›7RéméÇúQ™ê©nPCÙ¶õ¬ëğFÔ•fäåaZúS¯ş,mùQ«‘™³ã,l„ºŞöÆ|-ù«?ôınDú‹¨Ï$Û9ÿG= £1>ÁÆÌ”®MŞ_¤ÈˆLÇ|¨L<ŒÚüõZœ1Bˆ¡ˆˆˆF5~k8ë#jÀˆz“{³å÷FÁÿïíöé0␀␀␀␀IEND®B`‚# HID Device Configuration
# Define mouse and gamepad report formats for specific USB devices.
# When a device matching vid/pid is connected, this layout is used
# instead of auto-detection or boot protocol fallback.
#
# [[mouse]] fields:
#   vid, pid         - USB Vendor/Product ID (hex integers)
#   name             - Human-readable device name (for logging)
#   report_id        - Report ID byte value (-1 or omit if no report ID)
#   report_len       - Report data length in bytes (excluding Report ID)
#   skip_control_transfer - Skip USB control transfers for this device (default: false)
#   buttons          - Button field: offset (bits), size (bits)
#   x, y             - Axis fields: offset, size, min, max, relative
#   wheel            - Wheel field: offset, size, min, max, relative (OPTIONAL)
#   protocol         - "boot" (default) or "report" (OPTIONAL)
#
# The wheel is opt-in per device. Leave `wheel` out and the mouse behaves
# exactly as it always has, even if its descriptor has a wheel; write it and
# the notches are delivered to the focused window. Plug the mouse in and the
# log says whether it reports one, and at which offset:
#   usb_task: Mouse VID=0x.... PID=0x.... reports a wheel (offset=24 size=8); ...
# One notch of the wheel is 1, and turning it away from you is positive.
#
# A Boot Interface mouse answers with three bytes -- buttons, X, Y -- and its
# wheel is simply not among them, so no offset can reach it. protocol =
# "report" asks that one device for its own format instead, which is where its
# wheel lives. Only a device named here is asked; every other mouse keeps the
# boot path it has always taken. Use the HID Inspector to read the report the
# device then sends, because its layout is the device's own and not the
# standard three bytes. Over-declare report_len while measuring so the
# Inspector shows the whole report, then write the real length.
#
# Do not expect the report descriptor to help: for a Boot Interface device
# esp_lcd's HID host returns garbage for it (measured 2026-09-01), so the bits
# have to be measured from live reports.
#
# [[gamepad]] fields:
#   vid, pid         - USB Vendor/Product ID (hex integers)
#   name             - Human-readable device name (for logging)
#   report_len       - Report data length in bytes
#   buttons          - Button bitmask: offset (bits), size (bits)
#   hat              - HAT/D-pad field: offset (bits), size (bits) (optional)
#   left_x, left_y, right_x, right_y, l2, r2 - Axis fields: offset, size, center
#
# Bit offsets are counted from the start of report data (after Report ID if present).
# For signed relative axes, set min to negative value (e.g., min = -2048).
# For absolute axes (e.g., touchpad), set relative = false.

# Logitech Unifying Receiver - Report Protocol mode with 12-bit X/Y
[[mouse]]
vid = 0x046D
pid = 0xC534
name = "Logitech Unifying"
report_id = 0x02
report_len = 7
buttons = { offset = 0, size = 8, min = 0, max = 1, relative = false }
x = { offset = 16, size = 12, min = -2048, max = 2047, relative = true }
y = { offset = 28, size = 12, min = -2048, max = 2047, relative = true }

# Logickeyboard TITAN keyboard (integrated touchpad)
[[mouse]]
vid = 0x258A
pid = 0x1205
name = "TITAN keyboard touchpad"
report_id = 0x01
report_len = 5
buttons = { offset = 0, size = 8, min = 0, max = 1, relative = false }
x = { offset = 8, size = 8, min = -127, max = 127, relative = true }
y = { offset = 16, size = 8, min = -127, max = 127, relative = true }

# HORI PAD 3 TURBO (PS3 era gamepad, 19-byte report)
# Byte layout: [buttons_lo, buttons_hi, hat|0x0F, lx, ly, rx, ry, ...]
#
# Button mapping (verified):
#   0=Square  1=Cross  2=Circle  3=Triangle
#   4=L1  5=R1  6=L2  7=R2
#   8=SELECT  9=START  10=L3  11=R3
#   12=Up  13=Down  14=Left  15=Right  (from HAT switch)
#
# Axis mapping (verified, range -128 ~ 127):
#   0=Left stick X   1=Left stick Y
#   2=Right stick X  3=Right stick Y
#
[[gamepad]]
vid = 0x0F0D
pid = 0x0009
name = "HORI PAD 3 TURBO"
report_len = 19
buttons = { offset = 0, size = 16 }
hat = { offset = 16, size = 4 }
left_x = { offset = 24, size = 8, center = 128 }
left_y = { offset = 32, size = 8, center = 128 }
right_x = { offset = 40, size = 8, center = 128 }
right_y = { offset = 48, size = 8, center = 128 }

# Sipeed NanoKVM-USB - Absolute coordinates, 13-bit X/Y
[[mouse]]
vid = 0x359F
pid = 0x3302
name = "NanoKVM-USB"
report_id = 0x02
report_len = 6
skip_control_transfer = true
buttons = { offset = 0, size = 5, min = 0, max = 1, relative = false }
x = { offset = 8, size = 13, min = 0, max = 4095, relative = false }
y = { offset = 24, size = 13, min = 0, max = 4095, relative = false }

# Generic OEM "USB Optical Mouse" - 5-byte report, no Report ID,
# 12-bit X/Y packed (X spans byte1 + low nibble of byte2,
# Y spans high nibble of byte2 + byte3). Byte 4 is wheel (ignored).
# Verified with: 00 02 F0 FF 00 -> X=+2, Y=-1 (12-bit signed)
# TEMPORARILY DISABLED to test descriptor parse auto-detect path.
# [[mouse]]
# vid = 0x30FA
# pid = 0x0300
# name = "USB Optical Mouse (12-bit packed)"
# report_len = 5
# buttons = { offset = 0, size = 8, min = 0, max = 1, relative = false }
# x = { offset = 8, size = 12, min = -2048, max = 2047, relative = true }
# y = { offset = 20, size = 12, min = -2048, max = 2047, relative = true }

# MOSART 2.4G Keyboard Mouse (wireless combo dongle). Its Boot report is
# 3 bytes with no wheel, so it is asked for its own format: a Report ID of 3,
# 12-bit packed axes and the wheel in the fifth data byte. Measured with the
# HID Inspector -- 1 right gives 01 in raw byte 2, 1 down gives 10 in byte 3
# (a nibble up, which is what says the axes are 12 bits), a wheel notch gives
# FF in byte 5.
[[mouse]]
vid = 0x062A
pid = 0x4101
name = "MOSART 2.4G Mouse"
protocol = "report"
report_id = 0x03
report_len = 6
buttons = { offset = 0, size = 8, min = 0, max = 1, relative = false }
x = { offset = 8, size = 12, min = -2048, max = 2047, relative = true }
y = { offset = 20, size = 12, min = -2048, max = 2047, relative = true }
wheel = { offset = 32, size = 8, min = -127, max = 127, relative = true }
# System services for the browser build (/etc/services.toml in the bundle).
#
# Same shape and same meaning as config/services.toml, but only the entries
# that can work in a browser. The network services (net, timesync, tts) are
# left out: there is no WiFi to watch and no socket to reach an NTP or a
# synthesis server with, so listing them would buy a failure in the log at
# every boot and nothing else.
#
# The user's own list is /home/services.toml, exactly as on a machine. It is
# read after this one and wins field by field.

[clock]
file = "clock.rb"
class = "ClockService"
enable = true
interval_ms = 60000

# Rings a note on the hour, by subscribing to the clock/hour that the entry
# above publishes. The body is /usr/share/services/hourly_chime.rb and is the
# one to read before writing a service of your own -- which is why it is
# listed here at all. Off, as on a device: a page anyone may have open should
# not make a noise at a moment its reader did not choose. Turn it on in
# Services to hear it.
[hourly_chime]
file = "hourly_chime.rb"
class = "HourlyChimeService"
enable = false
# Settings for the WebAssembly (browser) build -- doc/wasm/.
#
# The web bundle stages ONLY git-tracked flash/ content plus this file, so
# nothing personal or secret can reach it. Based on system_conf_p4.toml: the
# wasm build reuses the Modern in-process display path (display_mode is not
# consulted on wasm; the value stays for the shared parser). No radios, no
# remote desktop.
system_name = "Family mruby OS (Web)"
display_width = 426
display_height = 240
# No overscan margin: the Tab5 LCD shows the full framebuffer, unlike
# the NTSC output where the border area is hidden by overscan.
display_margin_x = 0
display_margin_y = 0
default_user_app_width = 386
default_user_app_height = 180
display_mode = "tab5_dsi" # Tab5 MIPI-DSI panel, driven locally (ntsc_ipc / spi_direct / headless / tab5_dsi / sdl2 / atom_display)
# How many app slots to hand out. The device targets leave this out and get
# their build ceiling of 9; the web build raises the ceiling to 32
# (wasm/CMakeLists.txt) because browser memory is not the device's, and says so
# here. Anything above the ceiling is clamped with a warning at boot.
max_apps = 32
debug_mode = true
# No radios on the web build: BLE / WiFi settings are not present here.
mouse_scale_x = 0.5
mouse_scale_y = 0.5
# Rows one notch of the mouse wheel scrolls (1-20).
wheel_lines = 3
timezone = "JST-9"
keyboard_layout = "jp" # "jp" or "us"
# Internal RAM (KB) a spawn must leave for the rest of the machine; a start
# that would eat into this is refused with "Not enough memory". 30 is the
# floor that keeps drivers and networking able to allocate. 0 disables the
# floor (experiments only). Ignored by the Linux/wasm builds (host heap).
# CAUTION: too large locks out EVERY app - the editor and shell included -
# and the device then has no way to edit this file back (verified on a
# Tab5 at 200: free internal was ~127 KB, so nothing could start). Recovery
# is a reflash. Raise it in small steps.
# This is weighed against the free TOTAL only. A start is refused separately
# when no single free block holds the task stack; lowering this does not help
# that one (doc/reference/internal_ram_budget.md).
app_spawn_margin_kb = 30
language = "en" # "ja" or "en"

# The logo and the jingle at power-on. false skips both and saves about 2.7
# seconds -- for a machine that exists to run one thing, the ceremony is not
# wanted every time. Nothing else changes: the cursor, the status LED and
# startup_app below all happen either way.
boot_splash = true

# One app to open as soon as the desktop is up, by path (the same path the
# launcher uses, e.g. "/app/game/blockgame.app.rb"). Empty means none, which is
# the ordinary desktop. The browser sets this from ?app= in its URL.
startup_app = ""

# Desktop wallpaper. Empty means "whatever the theme says" -- the western
# picture, or the neon one under the cyberpunk theme, at the size of the
# screen. A path here wins over that; "none" leaves the desktop a plain
# colour. Any .png in /home/backgrounds shows up in Config's list.
wallpaper = ""

# Web default: the cyberpunk palette (RGB332). 0x01 is OFF-LIMITS for any
# theme color: it is the desktop foreground canvas color key (transparent).
# Config, inside the machine, is the only place these change; what it saves
# is handed back at the next boot (page_settings_wasm.c). The page had a
# theme selector of its own until 2026-09-03 -- doc/wasm/report/
# page_theme_removed.md says why it does not.
[theme]
desktop_bg = 0x00
menu_bg = 0x22
window_bg = 0x00
text = 0x1C
text_light = 0xFF
highlight = 0xE3
border = 0x0E
button = 0x46
dir_color = 0xFC

[[shortcuts]]
key = "l"
app = "launcher"

[[shortcuts]]
key = "s"
app = "default/shell"

[[shortcuts]]
key = "e"
app = "default/editor"

[[shortcuts]]
key = "n"
app = "app/tool/nsf_player.app.rb"

[[shortcuts]]
key = "i"
app = "default/inspector"

# App categories hidden from the launcher (files stay on flash and remain
# runnable via the editor / debugd). The Linux sim config has no entries,
# so the sim still shows everything.
[[launcher_exclude]]
dir = "debug"

[[launcher_exclude]]
dir = "test"

# The counterpart of the Retro configs' "modern" exclusion: apps that need
# hardware only a Retro machine has (NTSC output, the WROVER side) go under
# /app/retro and are hidden here. The directory does not exist yet -- an
# exclusion for a missing directory simply never matches. /app/modern stays
# visible on this board; that is what it is for.
[[launcher_exclude]]
dir = "retro"

# Remote desktop (browser viewer via WiFi). WiFi credentials live in
# /etc/wifi.toml (see config/wifi_p4.toml.example).
# Settings for the WebAssembly (browser) build -- doc/wasm/.
#
# The web bundle stages ONLY git-tracked flash/ content plus this file, so
# nothing personal or secret can reach it. Based on system_conf_p4.toml: the
# wasm build reuses the Modern in-process display path (display_mode is not
# consulted on wasm; the value stays for the shared parser). No radios, no
# remote desktop.
system_name = "Family mruby OS (Web)"
display_width = 426
display_height = 240
# No overscan margin: the Tab5 LCD shows the full framebuffer, unlike
# the NTSC output where the border area is hidden by overscan.
display_margin_x = 0
display_margin_y = 0
default_user_app_width = 386
default_user_app_height = 180
display_mode = "tab5_dsi" # Tab5 MIPI-DSI panel, driven locally (ntsc_ipc / spi_direct / headless / tab5_dsi / sdl2 / atom_display)
# How many app slots to hand out. The device targets leave this out and get
# their build ceiling of 9; the web build raises the ceiling to 32
# (wasm/CMakeLists.txt) because browser memory is not the device's, and says so
# here. Anything above the ceiling is clamped with a warning at boot.
max_apps = 32
debug_mode = true
# No radios on the web build: BLE / WiFi settings are not present here.
mouse_scale_x = 0.5
mouse_scale_y = 0.5
# Rows one notch of the mouse wheel scrolls (1-20).
wheel_lines = 3
timezone = "JST-9"
keyboard_layout = "jp" # "jp" or "us"
# Internal RAM (KB) a spawn must leave for the rest of the machine; a start
# that would eat into this is refused with "Not enough memory". 30 is the
# floor that keeps drivers and networking able to allocate. 0 disables the
# floor (experiments only). Ignored by the Linux/wasm builds (host heap).
# CAUTION: too large locks out EVERY app - the editor and shell included -
# and the device then has no way to edit this file back (verified on a
# Tab5 at 200: free internal was ~127 KB, so nothing could start). Recovery
# is a reflash. Raise it in small steps.
# This is weighed against the free TOTAL only. A start is refused separately
# when no single free block holds the task stack; lowering this does not help
# that one (doc/reference/internal_ram_budget.md).
app_spawn_margin_kb = 30
language = "en" # "ja" or "en"

# The logo and the jingle at power-on. false skips both and saves about 2.7
# seconds -- for a machine that exists to run one thing, the ceremony is not
# wanted every time. Nothing else changes: the cursor, the status LED and
# startup_app below all happen either way.
boot_splash = true

# One app to open as soon as the desktop is up, by path (the same path the
# launcher uses, e.g. "/app/game/blockgame.app.rb"). Empty means none, which is
# the ordinary desktop. The browser sets this from ?app= in its URL.
startup_app = ""

# Desktop wallpaper. Empty means "whatever the theme says" -- the western
# picture, or the neon one under the cyberpunk theme, at the size of the
# screen. A path here wins over that; "none" leaves the desktop a plain
# colour. Any .png in /home/backgrounds shows up in Config's list.
wallpaper = ""

# Web default: the cyberpunk palette (RGB332). 0x01 is OFF-LIMITS for any
# theme color: it is the desktop foreground canvas color key (transparent).
# Config, inside the machine, is the only place these change; what it saves
# is handed back at the next boot (page_settings_wasm.c). The page had a
# theme selector of its own until 2026-09-03 -- doc/wasm/report/
# page_theme_removed.md says why it does not.
[theme]
desktop_bg = 0x00
menu_bg = 0x22
window_bg = 0x00
text = 0x1C
text_light = 0xFF
highlight = 0xE3
border = 0x0E
button = 0x46
dir_color = 0xFC

[[shortcuts]]
key = "l"
app = "launcher"

[[shortcuts]]
key = "s"
app = "default/shell"

[[shortcuts]]
key = "e"
app = "default/editor"

[[shortcuts]]
key = "n"
app = "app/tool/nsf_player.app.rb"

[[shortcuts]]
key = "i"
app = "default/inspector"

# App categories hidden from the launcher (files stay on flash and remain
# runnable via the editor / debugd). The Linux sim config has no entries,
# so the sim still shows everything.
[[launcher_exclude]]
dir = "debug"

[[launcher_exclude]]
dir = "test"

# The counterpart of the Retro configs' "modern" exclusion: apps that need
# hardware only a Retro machine has (NTSC output, the WROVER side) go under
# /app/retro and are hidden here. The directory does not exist yet -- an
# exclusion for a missing directory simply never matches. /app/modern stays
# visible on this board; that is what it is for.
[[launcher_exclude]]
dir = "retro"

# Remote desktop (browser viewer via WiFi). WiFi credentials live in
# /etc/wifi.toml (see config/wifi_p4.toml.example).
# Array#all?

all?(*untyped patterns) -> bool

Whether every element passes

```ruby
ok = a.all? { |v| v > 0 }
```

# Array#at

at(Integer index) -> untyped

Get element at specified position

The same as `[]`.
```ruby
a.at(2)
```

# Array#clear

clear() -> Array

Remove all elements from array

Empties the array itself, so everyone holding it sees it empty.
```ruby
a.clear
```

# Array#collect!

collect!() { (untyped element) -> untyped } -> Array[untyped]

Execute block for each element and destructively replace

Rewrites the array in place instead of making a new one.
```ruby
a.collect! { |v| v * 2 }
```

# Array#count

count() -> Integer

Return the number of elements

The same as `length`.
```ruby
n = a.count
```

# Array#delete_at

delete_at(Integer index) -> untyped

Delete element at specified position

The rest shifts down, so deleting while looping forwards skips items --
go backwards.
```ruby
v = a.delete_at(0)
```

# Array#delete_if

delete_if() { (untyped element) -> untyped } -> self

Delete elements for which block returns true

```ruby
a.delete_if { |v| v.nil? }
```

# Array#each

each() { (untyped element) -> untyped } -> self

Execute block for each element

**A block call costs about 0.4ms.** In a per-frame loop, `while` with an
index is faster.
```ruby
a.each do |v|
  draw(v)
end
```

# Array#each_index

each_index() { (Integer index) -> untyped } -> self

Execute block for each index

```ruby
a.each_index { |i| p i }
```

# Array#empty?

empty?() -> bool

Check if array is empty

```ruby
return if a.empty?
```

# Array#first

first() -> untyped

Get first element

nil when empty.
```ruby
v = a.first
```

# Array#include?

include?(untyped object) -> bool

Check if element is included

It walks from the front, so it is slow on a long array; use a Hash when
you ask often.
```ruby
if a.include?(v)
```

# Array#inspect

inspect() -> String

Return string representation for debugging

```ruby
p a
```

# Array#join

join(?String separator) -> String

Join elements and return string

```ruby
a.join(", ")
```

# Array#last

last() -> untyped

Get last element

nil when empty.
```ruby
v = a.last
```

# Array#length

length() -> Integer

Return number of elements in array

```ruby
n = a.length
```

# Array#max

max() -> untyped

The largest element

nil when empty.
```ruby
v = a.max
```

# Array#min

min() -> untyped

The smallest element

nil when empty.
```ruby
v = a.min
```

# Array.new

new(?Integer size, ?untyped object) -> Array

Make an array

Reserving the size up front beats growing it item by item. The second
argument fills it.
```ruby
a = Array.new
b = Array.new(16, 0)
```

# Array#+

+(Array other) -> Array

Concatenate arrays

It makes a new array; to add on the end use `push`.
```ruby
c = a + b
```

# Array#&

&(Array other) -> self

Elements that are in both arrays

Keeps what is in both.
```ruby
[1, 2, 3] & [2, 3, 4]  #=> [2, 3]
```

# Array#[]

[](Integer index) -> untyped

Get element at specified position

**A negative index counts from the end.** Out of range gives nil, not an
error.
```ruby
a[0]
a[-1]
```

# Array#[]=

[]=(Integer index, untyped value) -> untyped

Set element at specified position

**`a[i], a[j] = a[j], a[i]` does not work** here -- a known picoruby gap.
Swap through a temporary instead.
```ruby
a[0] = 1
t = a[i]; a[i] = a[j]; a[j] = t
```

# Array#|

|(Array other) -> Array

The two arrays joined, without duplicates

Joins the two and drops the duplicates.
```ruby
[1, 2] | [2, 3]  #=> [1, 2, 3]
```

# Array#-

-(Array other) -> self

Return new array excluding specified elements

Drops everything the other one has.
```ruby
[1, 2, 3] - [2]  #=> [1, 3]
```

# Array#pop

pop(?Integer count) -> (untyped | self)

Remove and return last element

Takes from the **end**; with a count it returns that many as an array.
```ruby
v = a.pop
vs = a.pop(2)
```

# Array#push

push(untyped object) -> Array

Append element to end

The same as `<<`; it returns the array so calls can be chained.
```ruby
a.push(1)
a << 1
```

# Array#reject

reject() { (untyped element) -> untyped } -> self

Return array without elements the block matches

Drops the ones the block calls true; invert the test to say what to keep.
```ruby
a.reject { |v| v < 0 }
```

# Array#reject!

reject!() { (untyped element) -> untyped } -> self

Delete the elements the block matches, in place

Rewrites the array in place.
```ruby
a.reject! { |v| v.nil? }
```

# Array#shift

shift(?Integer count) -> (untyped | self)

Remove and return first element

Takes from the **front** -- a queue.
```ruby
v = a.shift
```

# Array#slice

slice(untyped index_or_range) -> untyped

Get subarray

```ruby
a.slice(1, 2)
```

# Array#sort

sort() -> self

Return sorted array

It raises when the values cannot be compared -- numbers mixed with
strings.
```ruby
b = a.sort
```

# Array#sort!

sort!() -> self

Sort destructively

Sorts in place.
```ruby
a.sort!
```

# Array#uniq

uniq() -> self

Return array with duplicates removed

Keeps the first of each; the order of first appearance is kept.
```ruby
b = a.uniq
```

# Array#uniq!

uniq!() -> self

Remove duplicates destructively

**nil when nothing changed.**
```ruby
a.uniq!
```

# Array#unshift

unshift(untyped object) -> Array

Prepend element to beginning

Puts it at the front, which shifts everything else along.
```ruby
a.unshift(0)
```
