# Hash#clear

clear() -> Hash

Remove all elements from hash

```ruby
h.clear
```

# Hash#delete

delete(untyped key) -> (untyped | nil)

Delete specified key

Returns what was there, or nil.
```ruby
v = h.delete(:name)
```

# Hash#each

each() { (untyped key, untyped value) -> untyped } -> self

Execute block for each key and value

**The block takes two**, the key and the value.
```ruby
h.each do |k, v|
  puts k.to_s + "=" + v.to_s
end
```

# Hash#empty?

empty?() -> bool

Check if hash is empty

```ruby
return if h.empty?
```

# Hash#has_key?

has_key?(untyped key) -> bool

Check if specified key exists

How you tell a key holding nil from a key that is not there.
```ruby
if h.has_key?(:name)
```

# Hash#has_value?

has_value?(untyped value) -> bool

Check if specified value exists

It walks the whole hash.
```ruby
if h.has_value?(v)
```

# Hash#inspect

inspect() -> String

Return string representation for debugging

```ruby
p h
```

# Hash#key

key(untyped value) -> (untyped | nil)

Look the key up from the value

Looks the key up from the value; nil when there is none.
```ruby
k = h.key(v)
```

# Hash#keys

keys() -> Array[untyped]

Return all keys as array

It builds a new array.
```ruby
h.keys.each { |k| p k }
```

# Hash#length

length() -> Integer

Return number of elements in hash

```ruby
n = h.length
```

# Hash#merge

merge(Hash other) -> self

Return new hash merged with another hash

**The other side wins on a shared key** -- what you want for overriding
settings.
```ruby
conf = defaults.merge(user)
```

# Hash#merge!

merge!(Hash other) -> self

Merge hash destructively

Merges into this hash.
```ruby
conf.merge!(user)
```

# Hash.new

new() -> Hash

Make an empty hash

**No default can be given**; a missing key is always nil.
```ruby
h = Hash.new
h = {}
```

# Hash#[]

[](untyped key) -> untyped

Get value for specified key

**A missing key gives nil**; it does not raise.
```ruby
h[:name]
h["name"]
```

# Hash#[]=

[]=(untyped key, untyped value) -> self

Set value for specified key

**A String key and a Symbol key are different.** Mixing them loses the
value.
```ruby
h[:name] = "a"
```

# Hash#==

==(untyped other) -> bool

Perform equality comparison

```ruby
if h == other
```

# Hash#to_h

to_h() -> self

Return self as hash

Returns itself.
```ruby
h.to_h
```

# Hash#values

values() -> Array[untyped]

Return all values as array

```ruby
h.values.each { |v| p v }
```
