# P5#alpha

alpha(Integer value) -> void

Set the transparency (does nothing yet)

**It does nothing today.** It is here so code copied from p5.js still runs;
use `blend_mode` to overlay.
```ruby
@p5.alpha(128)  # no effect
```

# P5#arc

arc(Integer cx, Integer cy, Integer r, Integer start_angle, Integer stop_angle) -> void

Draw an arc

**The angles are radians**, 0 at the right and going clockwise.
```ruby
@p5.arc(60, 60, 30,
        0, Math::PI / 2)
```

# P5#background

background(Integer color) -> void

Paint the whole canvas one colour

Call it at the top of a frame to wipe the last one. With a `blend_mode`
set it blends over instead of wiping.
```ruby
@p5.background(FmrbGfx::BLACK)
```

# P5#begin_shape

begin_shape() -> void

Start a free shape

Add corners with `vertex`, then close with `end_shape`.
```ruby
@p5.begin_shape
@p5.vertex(10, 10)
@p5.vertex(40, 20)
@p5.vertex(20, 40)
@p5.end_shape(true)
```

# P5#bezier

bezier(Integer x1, Integer y1, Integer x2, Integer y2, Integer x3, Integer y3, Integer x4, Integer y4) -> void

Draw a smooth curve

Start, two pull points, end. It is drawn as 20 straight pieces, so
`no_stroke` leaves nothing.
```ruby
@p5.bezier(10, 60, 30, 10,
           70, 10, 90, 60)
```

# P5#blend_mode

blend_mode(Integer mode) -> void

Set how colours are blended

`P5::ADD` brightens where shapes overlap, `P5::XOR` inverts; 0 is normal.
```ruby
@p5.blend_mode(P5::ADD)
```

# P5#circle

circle(Integer cx, Integer cy, Integer r) -> void

Draw a circle

Given by its **centre** and radius.
```ruby
@p5.circle(60, 60, 20)
```

# P5#color

color(Integer r, Integer g, Integer b) -> Integer

Make a colour from red, green and blue

Squeezes three 0-255 values into one byte: 8 levels of red and green,
4 of blue.
```ruby
c = @p5.color(255, 128, 0)
```

# P5#curve

curve(Integer x1, Integer y1, Integer x2, Integer y2, Integer x3, Integer y3, Integer x4, Integer y4) -> void

Draw a curve through points

Only the stretch between the middle two points is drawn; the outer two
just shape the bend.
```ruby
@p5.curve(0, 60, 10, 60,
          90, 60, 100, 60)
```

# P5#ellipse

ellipse(Integer cx, Integer cy, Integer rx, Integer ry) -> void

Draw an ellipse

```ruby
@p5.ellipse(60, 60, 30, 15)
```

# P5#end_shape

end_shape() -> void

Finish the free shape

**Pass true to close the shape.** Filling needs three corners or more.
```ruby
@p5.end_shape(true)
```

# P5#fill

fill(Integer color) -> void

Set the fill colour

The inside colour of every shape from here on, as a one-byte RGB332 value.
```ruby
@p5.fill(FmrbGfx::RED)
@p5.circle(60, 60, 20)
```

# P5#get_pixel

get_pixel(Integer x, Integer y) -> Integer

Read one pixel's colour

It waits for an answer, so it is slow. Do not read it inside a loop.
```ruby
c = @p5.get_pixel(10, 20)
```

# P5#height

height() -> Integer

Canvas height in pixels

```ruby
cy = @p5.height / 2
```

# P5#image

image(Integer image_id, Integer x, Integer y) -> void

Draw a loaded image

The id is one an `FmrbGfx` load returned; the 4th and 5th arguments
scale it.
```ruby
@p5.image(id, 10, 20)
@p5.image(id, 10, 20, 2.0, 2.0)
```

# P5#image_masked

image_masked(Integer image_id, String mask_data, Integer x, Integer y, Integer w, Integer h) -> void

Draw an image through a mask

The mask is one bit per pixel; the image shows only where the bit is 1.
```ruby
@p5.image_masked(id, mask,
                 10, 20, 16, 16)
```

# P5#line

line(Integer x0, Integer y0, Integer x1, Integer y1) -> void

Draw a line

```ruby
@p5.line(0, 0, 100, 60)
```

# P5.new

new(FmrbGfx gfx) -> P5

Make the p5-style drawing tools

It only wraps an `FmrbGfx`, so you can draw on the same canvas with both.
```ruby
@p5 = P5.new(@gfx)
```

# P5#no_fill

no_fill() -> void

Draw nothing inside

Leaves an outline only.
```ruby
@p5.no_fill
@p5.rect(10, 10, 40, 20)
```

# P5#no_stroke

no_stroke() -> void

Draw no outline

Leaves a filled shape with no outline.
```ruby
@p5.no_stroke
```

# P5#point

point(Integer x, Integer y) -> void

Set one pixel

Uses the **stroke** colour; nothing appears after `no_stroke`.
```ruby
@p5.point(10, 20)
```

# P5#pop_matrix

pop_matrix() -> void

Go back to the remembered transform

Doing it with an empty stack is harmless.
```ruby
@p5.pop_matrix
```

# P5#present

present() -> void

Show what has been drawn

**Nothing appears until you call it** -- once at the end of a frame.
```ruby
@p5.present
```

# P5#push_matrix

push_matrix() -> void

Remember the current transform

**Always pair it with `pop_matrix`**, or the shift is left behind.
```ruby
@p5.push_matrix
```

# P5#quad

quad(Integer x0, Integer y0, Integer x1, Integer y1, Integer x2, Integer y2, Integer x3, Integer y3) -> void

Draw a quadrilateral from four points

The points are joined in order; crossing them over breaks the shape.
```ruby
@p5.quad(10, 10, 50, 14,
         48, 40, 12, 36)
```

# P5#rect

rect(Integer x, Integer y, Integer w, Integer h) -> void

Draw a rectangle

Given by its **top-left corner** and its size.
```ruby
@p5.rect(10, 10, 40, 20)
```

# P5#reset_matrix

reset_matrix() -> void

Clear every transform

```ruby
@p5.reset_matrix
```

# P5#rotate

rotate(Integer angle) -> void

Turn what is drawn

In **radians**, about the origin. To spin around an object, `translate`
there first.
```ruby
@p5.translate(60, 60)
@p5.rotate(Math::PI / 4)
```

# P5#scale

scale(Integer sx) -> void

Scale what is drawn

A **second argument** scales the two axes apart; 1.0 is life size.
```ruby
@p5.scale(2.0)
@p5.scale(2.0, 0.5)
```

# P5#set_pixel

set_pixel(Integer x, Integer y, Integer color) -> void

Set one pixel

**Not put through the transform** -- these are raw screen coordinates.
```ruby
@p5.set_pixel(10, 20, c)
```

# P5#stroke

stroke(Integer color) -> void

Set the outline colour

```ruby
@p5.stroke(FmrbGfx::WHITE)
```

# P5#stroke_weight

stroke_weight(Integer w) -> void

Set the outline thickness

A thick line is drawn as several thin ones, so it costs more.
```ruby
@p5.stroke_weight(3)
```

# P5#text

text(String str, Integer x, Integer y) -> void

Draw text

The colour comes from **`text_color`**, not `fill`, and the position
follows `text_align`.
```ruby
@p5.text("Hello", 10, 20)
```

# P5#text_align

text_align(Symbol horizontal) -> void

Set how text is aligned

Horizontally `:left`, `:center`, `:right`; the **second argument** takes
`:top`, `:center`, `:bottom`.
```ruby
@p5.text_align(:center, :center)
```

# P5#text_color

text_color(Integer color) -> void

Set the text colour

```ruby
@p5.text_color(FmrbGfx::WHITE)
```

# P5#text_font

text_font(Symbol font) -> void

Choose the font

`:default` is ASCII; **`:ja` draws Japanese** (at size 8).
```ruby
@p5.text_font(:ja)
@p5.text("ã“ã‚“ã«ã¡ã¯", 10, 20)
```

# P5#text_leading

text_leading(Integer pixels) -> void

Set the space between lines

```ruby
@p5.text_leading(12)
```

# P5#text_width

text_width(String str) -> Integer

Measure how wide text would be

Measured in the font currently chosen; use it to centre text yourself.
```ruby
w = @p5.text_width(s)
```

# P5#translate

translate(Integer tx, Integer ty) -> void

Shift where things are drawn

It shifts **everything drawn afterwards**; pair it with `push_matrix` to
get back.
```ruby
@p5.push_matrix
@p5.translate(60, 60)
@p5.circle(0, 0, 10)
@p5.pop_matrix
```

# P5#triangle

triangle(Integer x0, Integer y0, Integer x1, Integer y1, Integer x2, Integer y2) -> void

Draw a triangle

```ruby
@p5.triangle(10, 50, 30, 10,
             50, 50)
```

# P5#vertex

vertex(Integer x, Integer y) -> void

Add a corner to the shape

Called before `begin_shape` it is silently dropped.
```ruby
@p5.vertex(40, 20)
```

# P5#width

width() -> Integer

Canvas width in pixels

```ruby
cx = @p5.width / 2
```
